@prefix triage: <urn:atm:triage:> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<urn:atm:triage:finding/COMPOSE-ACK-RELOCATED>
  a triage:Finding ;
  triage:findingId "COMPOSE-ACK-RELOCATED" ;
  triage:title "compose-vs-ack decision relocated (not collapsed) into atm-core, plus a second independent construction site in the daemon dispatcher" ;
  triage:description """The dispatcher-local compose-vs-ack branch (request.acknowledges_message_id.is_some() && request.source_remote_host.is_none()) was deleted from crates/atm-daemon/src/runtime_health/dispatch_delivery.rs in AG.21, but the identical decision now lives in two locations instead of being collapsed:

(1) crates/atm-core/src/send/mod.rs:365-383 (execute_outbound_send_with_runtime_and_post_send_emitter): Contains the relocated fork at line 371, branching to ack_mail_with_runtime_and_post_send_emitter (returns SendResponseEnvelope::Acknowledged) vs send_mail_with_runtime_and_post_send_emitter (returns SendResponseEnvelope::Sent). This is documented by AG18-QA-003 as "open-relocated" (moved from daemon to core, but the fork not collapsed).

(2) crates/atm-daemon/src/runtime_health/dispatch_delivery.rs:74-98: Contains a second, independent construction site that bypasses the canonical send/mod.rs entrypoint entirely. Lines 77 and 90 directly construct SendResponseEnvelope::Sent for RemoteSendDeliveryOutcome::Deferred and RemoteSendDeliveryOutcome::OutcomeUnknown remote outcomes, forming a separate decision fork that would not be addressed by fixing the send/mod.rs relocation alone.

The architectural violation is not collapsed: the canonical decision (send vs ack, determined by a boolean flag check) exists in TWO independent call sites (send/mod.rs and dispatch_delivery.rs), and the daemon-layer call site bypasses the core-layer canonical function entirely, preventing a single unified fix. This is the ladder's 4th confirmed "relocated, not collapsed" recurrence (prior occurrences: AG18-QA-002, AG18-QA-003 fork relocation, AG18-QA-004 caller-side duplication).""" ;
  triage:phaseId "phase-AG" ;
  triage:triageMode "initial_pass" ;
  triage:repeatable true ;
  triage:sweepScope "workspace" ;
  triage:category "architecture" ;
  triage:severity "blocking" ;
  triage:status "open" ;
  triage:dispatchReady true ;
  triage:triagedAt "2026-07-19T09:15:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
  triage:hasOccurrence <urn:atm:triage:occurrence/COMPOSE-ACK-RELOCATED/pAG-s21/1> ;
  triage:hasOccurrence <urn:atm:triage:occurrence/COMPOSE-ACK-RELOCATED/pAG-s21/2> ;
  triage:hasOccurrence <urn:atm:triage:occurrence/COMPOSE-ACK-RELOCATED/pAG-s21/3> ;
  triage:openOn <urn:atm:triage:worktree/pAG-s21/191c198b> ;
  triage:promoteTo <urn:atm:triage:worktree/pAG-s21/191c198b> ;
  triage:notes """Occurrence 1 is the relocated fork in send/mod.rs:371-382, already documented by AG18-QA-003 as "open-relocated". Occurrences 2 and 3 are the second, independent construction site in dispatch_delivery.rs:77 and :90 that directly construct SendResponseEnvelope::Sent for remote deferred/unknown outcomes, bypassing the canonical entrypoint and forming a second, independent architectural violation (not just a duplication of the first). The full workspace sweep on AG.21 confirms no occurrence on lower branches (AG.18-AG.20) has this specific bypass pattern yet; it was introduced when dispatch_delivery.rs was refactored to separate local vs remote routing. This finding overlaps with AG18-QA-002 and AG18-QA-003 but specifically clarifies that there are TWO distinct construction-site issues that together form a "relocated but not collapsed" pattern. AG21-FIX-1 is currently in progress on this branch.""" .

